    {
      "question": "e-21665",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "5b059f4f98f3a83f891a97c4da9f86570fc432a18b7a6502c93abd3425debc12",
      "new_sha256": "f0b1972fc5e588e38bb5806dfd7f960522bca1430490b821b1137d99d41e0efc",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-21665",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{\u00a0}4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/assets/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21665.json",
          "file_sha256": "a6913b5f1ad1f454fcf7c4244ca05b7a166777f9e1b3e63d791ddb9e6994809e"
        }
      ],
